{"id":"deep-fermat-1","title":"Can cheap AI labs under OSO prove Fermat from scratch?: Fermat's little theorem, depth 11, one OSO α 0.2 run, 4 labs, $2 a lab, 100 rounds (Nov 23)","question":"Can four cheap AI labs prove Fermat's little theorem from scratch, 95 hidden steps deep, when they share every result at once and credit flows back (OSO, α 0.2)?","answer":"Yes. The four labs proved Fermat's little theorem from scratch in round 61, for $2.53 of the $8: 11 levels deep, resting on 53 results from all four labs. One run with nothing to compare: it shows the depth is reachable, not that OSO's rules helped.","results":{"heading":"Deep set: Fermat's little theorem from scratch (one run, 2026-11-23 to 24)","html":"<p><a href=\"https://github.com/open-science-org/experiment/blob/master/results/community/deep-fermat-1/summary.md\">Result</a>. <strong>Setup:</strong> OSO α 0.2 (public at once, α 0.2, m 0, m_bit 0, f 0). 4 DeepSeek V4.1 Flash labs, $2 a lab, up to 100 rounds, labs cannot stop early. One run, no other setup: it shows what a community can reach, not whether OSO&#39;s rules help. The deep set has its own numbers built from zero (no Mathlib) and one posted target, P1, Fermat&#39;s little theorem, at depth 11; the 95 steps below it are hidden. Stand-in labs replaying the reference proofs reach P1 in round 27.</p>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong> P1 was proved in round 61, for $2.53 at the reference price (about a third of the $8; the run ends when the target is proved). The proof rests on 53 results, from all 4 labs. Labs proved 82 lemmas; 63 results built on another lab&#39;s work. Lean matched 50 of the 95 hidden steps to lab lemmas as stated (steps proved in another form or split over several lemmas are not counted).</p>\n<p><strong>The bottleneck:</strong> from round 25 the labs kept trying one step, (k+1)·C(n+1,k+1) = (n+1)·C(n,k), and failed 57 times (counting tries of this statement and its close variants). Lab 1 proved it in round 59. In round 60 lab 4 used it to show that a prime p divides C(p,k), and in round 61 it proved P1.</p>\n<p><strong>Harness:</strong> 245 of 568 replies (43%) ran out of length and gave no answer; 28 turns were lost to provider errors. The laptop slept during rounds 8 to 15, which only slowed the run. This run started before the payout ledger existed (2026-11-24), so its earnings cannot be audited.</p>\n"},"updatedAt":1795644387323,"startedAt":1795504075540,"problems":"deep","moneyPerRun":8,"targets":[{"id":"P1","depth":11,"bounty":4096,"area":"P","text":"Fermat's little theorem, from scratch: for every prime p and every number a, a to the power p leaves the same remainder as a when divided by p.","statement":"{p : N} (hp : Prime p) (a : N) : ModEq p (a ^ p) a"}],"about":{"labs":4,"models":["deepseek-v4.1-flash"],"budget":2,"rounds":100,"mayStop":false,"repairs":2,"hidden":95,"areas":["Numbers from scratch (deep set): addition, multiplication, order, divisibility, primes, binomial coefficients and congruences, built from zero and successor."]},"dag":[{"id":"P-01","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-02","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-03","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-04","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-05","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-06","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-07","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-08","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-09","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-10","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-11","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-12","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-13","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-14","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-15","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]},{"id":"P-16","depth":1,"area":"P","posted":false,"uses":[]}